"""
data_reconciliation.py
=======================
Row-level / column-VALUE reconciliation between

    core             (production read-replica)   -> "source"
    core_tst_std001  (test CDA)                  -> "target"

schema ods_views. Table names are now IDENTICAL in both catalogs
(vw_<system>_current_<xx>_<name>), so tables are paired on
(source_system, base_table_name). Tables ending _sa or _cda are excluded
from BOTH catalogs.

ROW KEY: a column named "id" (case-insensitive) present on BOTH sides.
Tables without it are NOT row-compared (strategy NO_KEY_SKIP) but still get
a count_status.

SCOPE / STRATEGY PER TABLE (auto-selected)
    DATE_FILTERED  table has updatetime / createtime / eventtimestamp /
                   azure_load_date (first found, on BOTH sides): only rows
                   inside the batch window are compared.
                   Window = (today CUTOFF_HOUR:00 - LOOKBACK_DAYS days) ->
                            (today CUTOFF_HOUR:00).
                   Set LOOKBACK_DAYS = 1 for a pure daily 3AM-3AM window.
    FULL_HASH      no such column, source count < SMALL_TABLE_ROW_THRESHOLD:
                   whole table compared every run.
    ID_WATERMARK   no such column, bigger table, integral id: only ids above
                   the last reconciled id are compared (new rows only -
                   edits to old rows are NOT seen by this strategy).
    NO_KEY_SKIP    no usable id on both sides: counts only.

FALSE-MISMATCH PROTECTION (the 1 vs 1.0 problem)
    Values are NOT compared as raw strings. Each column is normalized to a
    canonical form chosen from the datatypes on BOTH sides:
      NUMERIC          INT/LONG/FLOAT/DOUBLE/DECIMAL on either side ->
                       DECIMAL(38,NUMERIC_SCALE) text, so 1, 1.0, 1.00 and
                       0.1+0.2 vs 0.3 all compare equal
      TEMPORAL         DATE/TIMESTAMP -> 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS'
                       (a DATE equals a timestamp at midnight)
      STRING           trimmed; empty string treated as NULL (configurable)
      NUMERIC_STRING   string side parsed as a number when it is one ("1.0")
      TEMPORAL_STRING  string side parsed as a timestamp when it is one
      BOOLEAN          boolean vs boolean only. Boolean-like values are NOT
                       treated as interchangeable across types: source 'N' vs
                       target 0, or true vs 1, is REPORTED as a mismatch.
      BINARY -> hex, COMPLEX -> to_json, anything else -> raw string compare
    NULLs are hashed with an explicit sentinel, so (a, NULL, b) and
    (a, b, NULL) can no longer collide.
    The audit columns in EXCLUDED_COLUMNS_FROM_DATA are never compared.

FORMAT vs VALUE DIFFERENCES
    A second hash over the RAW text of every column is taken alongside the
    normalized one. Where the normalized values agree but the raw text
    differs (e.g. '2.0' vs '2', '1.74234224E8' vs '174234224', DATE vs
    midnight TIMESTAMP, NULL vs '') the row is a FORMAT difference: counted
    and listed in the comments, but NOT a failure. Where the normalized values
    differ it is a VALUE mismatch - the real issue. The comments column lists,
    per column, "n row(s) differ [x VALUE, y FORMAT]" with example ids
    (counts come from a capped sample of ids on large tables and say so).

HOW IT STAYS FAST ON LARGE TABLES
    * One narrow (key, 64-bit xxhash) projection per side, aggregated per key
      and joined once - no wide row shuffles, no full-row comparison.
    * Column-by-column diff runs ONLY for a capped sample of mismatching ids
      (DETAIL_MAX_IDS_PER_TABLE), pulled with a pushed-down id IN (...)
      filter so Delta file skipping applies.
    * Tables run in parallel (MAX_PARALLEL_TABLES threads) as independent
      Spark jobs; a failure in one table is recorded and does not stop the run.
    * Metadata (tables/columns) is read once per catalog, not per table.

OUTPUTS (Delta, partitioned by run_date, append + is_latest_for_day +
RETENTION_DAYS purge, same pattern as the other scripts)
    SUMMARY_OUTPUT_TABLE : one row per table
    DETAIL_OUTPUT_TABLE  : one row per (id, column) mismatch (sampled), plus
                           '<ROW>' rows for sampled ids missing on one side
    WATERMARK_TABLE      : last reconciled id per ID_WATERMARK table

count_status values (rows in scope, per side):
    COUNT MATCH | COUNT MISMATCH - <catalog> HIGHER by N | NO DATA |
    TABLE MISSING in <catalog>
"""

